  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Point at the VSSDK build tools this repo pins, not at the ones belonging to whichever Visual
      Studio happens to be installed. The import at the bottom of this file resolves
      $(VSToolsPath)\VSSDK\Microsoft.VsSDK.targets, and that targets file is what constructs the
      VsixUtil command line - while Microsoft.VSSDK.BuildTools.targets supplies the VsixUtil.exe
      that has to parse it. Letting those two come from different releases is a version diagonal:

        VS 18's Microsoft.VsSDK.targets passes Is64BitBuild="true", hardcoded and with no property
        to turn it off, and VsixUtil.exe 15.8.3247 answers "'-is64BitBuild' is an invalid argument"
        and exits -1.

      The pinned package ships its own tools\VSSDK\Microsoft.VsSDK.targets, so both halves now come
      from 15.8.3247 and agree with each other. This also stops the produced .vsix depending on
      which Visual Studio the machine has, which for a servicing branch is the property that matters.

      This sits AFTER the Dependencies.props import on purpose: $(VSSdkBuildToolsVersion) is defined
      there, and a default written above it expands to an empty version and resolves to a path that
      does not exist.

      The path is NORMALISED TO ABSOLUTE rather than left relative, and that is not tidiness.
      Create-VsixPackage copies this project from src\package\VSIXProject to artifacts\<config>\VSIX
      and builds the copy, so a relative path is interpreted from a directory the author never sees.
      The pinned package's own Microsoft.VSSDK.BuildTools.props sets VSToolsPath to an absolute
      $(ThisPackageDirectory)\tools for the same reason - but that props file is never imported here,
      see the finding recorded below, so this project has to do it itself.

      WHAT ELSE THAT PROPS FILE WOULD HAVE SET, AND WHY NOT SETTING IT IS SAFE. Raised by the 15.9
      line, which runs the other arrangement and asked the question of the line actually running this
      one. Measured here against the pinned package on disk rather than reasoned:

        Microsoft.VSSDK.BuildTools.props sets 5 properties
          ThisPackageDirectory, VSToolsPath, VsSDKInstall, VsSDKIncludes, VsSDKToolsPath

        references in tools\VSSDK\Microsoft.VsSDK.targets
          VSToolsPath      0        VsSDKIncludes    0     <- dead
          VsSDKInstall     3        VsSDKToolsPath   0     <- dead
          OutputPath       2        positive control, so the zeros are measurements

      All three VsSDKInstall references are inside Target RunRegRiched, which registers RichEdit for
      VS integration scenarios. That target IS on the build chain - PrepareForBuildDependsOn appends
      it - but it carries Condition="'$(RunRegRiched)' == 'true'", and that property is set nowhere:
      not in the targets, not in the props, not in this project. So the target never executes and
      VsSDKInstall being empty is UNREACHABLE here, not merely latent.

      Were it ever reached, the consequence is a WARNING and not an execution: the Warning carries
      !Exists(...) and fires, while the Exec carries the NON-negated Exists(...) and is skipped. An
      empty path cannot be executed by this target. Worth stating because the opposite reading - that
      it would Exec an empty path - is the natural one and is what the two lines first assumed.

      Its FindSDKInstallation dependency resolves inside the same pinned package,
      tools\vssdk\Microsoft.VisualStudio.Sdk.Common.targets, so nothing dangles.

      EMPIRICAL, which outranks all of the above: this arrangement has produced a VSIX in CI three
      times since it landed in 3965b7a14 on 2026-09-01 - builds 3064282, 3064344 and 3064417.

      DO NOT "FIX" THIS BY SETTING THE THREE PROPERTIES. Two are referenced nowhere and the third is
      unreachable, so it would change no behaviour while adding divergence from the shipped project.
    -->
    <VSToolsPath Condition="'$(VSToolsPath)' == ''">$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)', '$(TestPlatformRoot)', 'packages', 'microsoft.vssdk.buildtools', '$(VSSdkBuildToolsVersion)', 'tools'))</VSToolsPath>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!--
    FINDING, RECORDED AND DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED. The condition below reads
    '($TestPlatformRoot)packages\...' - parenthesis and dollar transposed - which is literal text that
    never exists, so these props have never been imported by this project: not now, and not in 2021.
    The .targets import near the bottom of the file spells the same condition correctly, so the two
    halves of one package are imported inconsistently.

    Correcting it would not restore 2021 behaviour, it would depart from it. The malformed condition
    never matched, so the shipped .vsix was produced WITHOUT these props, and a build that imports
    them does something this project has never done. On a servicing branch whose whole purpose is to
    reproduce what shipped, the typo is load-bearing.

    It is left in place on purpose, and the VSIX builds without it. If some later change genuinely
    needs these props, that is a deliberate departure and it has to be paid for with a comparison of
    the produced .vsix against the shipped one.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(TestPlatformRoot)packages\microsoft.vssdk.buildtools\$(VSSdkBuildToolsVersion)\build\Microsoft.VSSDK.BuildTools.props" Condition="Exists('($TestPlatformRoot)packages\microsoft.vssdk.buildtools\$(VSSdkBuildToolsVersion)\build\Microsoft.VSSDK.BuildTools.props')" />
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildExtensionsPath)\$(MSBuildToolsVersion)\Microsoft.Common.props" Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildExtensionsPath)\$(MSBuildToolsVersion)\Microsoft.Common.props')" />
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Configuration Condition=" '$(Configuration)' == '' ">Debug</Configuration>
    <Platform Condition=" '$(Platform)' == '' ">AnyCPU</Platform>
    <SchemaVersion>2.0</SchemaVersion>
    <ProjectTypeGuids>{82b43b9b-a64c-4715-b499-d71e9ca2bd60};{FAE04EC0-301F-11D3-BF4B-00C04F79EFBC}</ProjectTypeGuids>
    <ProjectGuid>{0625257F-549C-4558-97ED-C79EC43C3480}</ProjectGuid>
    <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
    <AppDesignerFolder>Properties</AppDesignerFolder>
    <RootNamespace>TestPlatform</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>TestPlatform</AssemblyName>
    <!--
      v4.6 targeting is no longer installable: on a current machine
      Reference Assemblies\...\.NETFramework\v4.6 exists but holds 126 files and no mscorlib.dll,
      where v4.6.2 holds 258 with one, and MSBuild answers MSB3644. Nothing in the produced VSIX
      depends on this value: the project has no source files, IncludeAssemblyInVSIXContainer and
      CopyBuildOutputToOutputDirectory are both false, so it compiles nothing and contributes no
      assembly to the container. The framework version is here only so reference resolution can
      complete, and 4.6.2 is the nearest version that still ships a targeting pack.
    -->
    <TargetFrameworkVersion>v4.6.2</TargetFrameworkVersion>
    <GeneratePkgDefFile>false</GeneratePkgDefFile>
    <IncludeAssemblyInVSIXContainer>false</IncludeAssemblyInVSIXContainer>
    <IncludeDebugSymbolsInVSIXContainer>false</IncludeDebugSymbolsInVSIXContainer>
    <IncludeDebugSymbolsInLocalVSIXDeployment>false</IncludeDebugSymbolsInLocalVSIXDeployment>
    <CopyBuildOutputToOutputDirectory>false</CopyBuildOutputToOutputDirectory>
    <CopyOutputSymbolsToOutputDirectory>false</CopyOutputSymbolsToOutputDirectory>
    <DeployExtension>False</DeployExtension>
    <IsProductComponent>true</IsProductComponent>
    <VsixInputFileLocation>$(TestPlatformRoot)artifacts\$(Configuration)\net451\win7-x64</VsixInputFileLocation>
    <OutputPath>$(TestPlatformRoot)artifacts\$(Configuration)\VSIX</OutputPath>
    <ExtensionInstallationFolder>TestPlatform</ExtensionInstallationFolder>
    <ExtensionInstallationRelativeToVS>[installDir]\Common7\IDE\Extensions\TestPlatform</ExtensionInstallationRelativeToVS>

    <!-- Disable warning that there are no source files. It is intentional. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);2008</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>
